\paragraph{Note on the model file.} ngspice reports that the model parameters \texttt{XL} and \texttt{XW} are not recognised and ignores
them. In this file $XL = 0$ and $XW = -10$\,nm, so the effect is a 10\,nm width change and is negligible
for the widths used here.


%===================================================================================
\section{Hand design: the LDO design procedure}\label{sec:hand}
The sizing follows the system-level design procedure of Rinc\'on-Mora: the design considerations of
\cite[Chapter~II]{rm_thesis} (AC analysis, transient analysis, pass device, amplifier), the
pass-element choice of \cite[Section~6.1]{rm_thesis}, and the dropout, buffer and gain-stage
equations of \cite[Sections~II--IV]{rm_jssc}. Each formula is used in the form proved in Part~1. The
script \texttt{design\_flow.py} carries out the eight steps below, and each number is quoted from its
output. Steps~6 and~7 run twice. \emph{Pass~A} uses square-law parameters only. \emph{Pass~B} replaces
$g_m/I_D$ and $\lambda$ by the values read at the simulated operating point of the previous design
(v3, Section~\ref{sec:iter}), the usual second pass of a hand design. The result is the final design v4.

\paragraph{Step 1 -- Topology.} Of the five pass elements compared in \cite[Table~6.1]{rm_thesis}, the
common-source PMOS has the lowest dropout ($V_{SD,sat}$) together with a low quiescent current, and the
reference calls it the best overall choice, so $M_P$ is a PMOS. Its large gate capacitance must not load the high-impedance gain stage, so a buffer isolates the
two (the buffered architecture of \cite[Fig.~2.7]{rm_thesis}). The gain stage is the five-transistor OTA
of \cite[Fig.~8(a)]{rm_jssc}, which needs only a small common-mode range around $\VREF$
\cite[Section~IV]{rm_jssc}. With $\VREF = <<SP['VREF']|.1f>>$\,V a PMOS input pair is used (Step~6a). The buffer is a
class-A PMOS source follower $M_B$ biased by $M_{BS}$, with its bulk tied to its source.

\paragraph{Step 2 -- Feedback network} \cite[Section~2.1]{rm_thesis}. The divider current is a
quiescent-current overhead, so it is kept small: $I_R = <<F['s2']['I_R']*1e6|.0f>>\,\mu$A. Then
$\beta = \VREF/\VOUT = <<F['s2']['beta']|.2f>>$, $R_2 = \VREF/I_R = <<F['s2']['R2']/1e3|.0f>>$\,k$\Omega$ and
$R_1 = (\VOUT-\VREF)/I_R = <<F['s2']['R1']/1e3|.0f>>$\,k$\Omega$.

\paragraph{Step 3 -- Pass device} \cite[Eqs.~(12)--(14)]{rm_jssc}, \cite[Section~2.3]{rm_thesis}. Two
conditions bound $W/L$ from below.
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0.1ex]
\item \emph{Dropout.} The buffer can pull $\VG$ down to $V_{G,min} = V_{OV4} + \VSGB \approx <<F['s3']['vg_min']|.2f>>$\,V
($\VSGB$ is checked in Step~6b). At $V_{IN,min}$ the available drive is
$\VSGP = <<F['s3']['vsg_avail']|.3f>>$\,V, i.e.\ $\ov = <<F['s3']['vov_avail']|.3f>>$\,V. Carrying $I_{max}$ at
$\VSD = V_{DO}$ (triode, \eqref{T-eq:vov_do}, the exact form of \cite[Eq.~(12)]{rm_jssc}) needs
$W/L \ge <<F['s3']['wl_do']|.0f>>$.
\item \emph{Saturation at $V_{IN,nom}$.} The loop gain and the PSRR collapse in triode
(Corollary~\ref{T-cor:dropout_psrr}), so $M_P$ must stay saturated at the nominal input.
By \eqref{T-eq:wl_sat} with a 10\,mV margin, $W/L \ge <<F['s3']['wl_sat']|.0f>>$.
\end{itemize}
The saturation condition is binding. Rounding up to fingers of 10\,$\mu$m gives
$M_P = <<F['s3']['MP']|d>>\times10\,\mu$m$/0.18\,\mu$m ($W/L = <<F['s3']['WL']|.0f>>$). With this size the dropout point needs
only $\VSGP^{DO} = <<F['s3']['vsg_do']|.3f>>$\,V, below the available <<F['s3']['vsg_avail']|.3f>>\,V, and the on-resistance at
the available drive is $R_{on} = 1/(\KP\frac WL\ov) = <<F['s3']['ron']|.2f>>\,\Omega$ \cite[Eq.~(13)]{rm_jssc}. At full load
$\VSGP = <<F['s3']['vsg_max']|.3f>>$\,V and $\gmP = <<F['s3']['gm_p_max']|.3f>>$\,S. At light load (weak inversion) the
simulated $I_D$--$V_{SG}$ curve of one finger gives $\VSGP = <<F['s3']['vsg_min']|.3f>>$\,V and
$\gmP = <<F['s3']['gm_p_min']*1e3|.2f>>$\,mS, so $\Delta\VSGP = <<F['s3']['dVSG']*1e3|.0f>>$\,mV. The gate
capacitance, $C_{par}$ in \cite{rm_thesis,rm_jssc}, is $C_G = \tfrac23WLC_{ox}+2WC_{ov} = <<F['s3']['Cpar']*1e12|.2f>>$\,pF.

\paragraph{Step 4 -- Open-loop gain from regulation and PSRR} \cite[Eq.~(2.9)]{rm_thesis}. Equation~(2.9)
of \cite{rm_thesis} gives the closed-loop output resistance $R_{o,pass}/(1+A_{ol}\beta)\approx 1/(G\gmP)$
with $G = \beta\Aea\Ab$. Since $\gmP$ changes by two decades over the load range, it is integrated over
the range, which is Corollary~\ref{T-cor:static}(b): $|\Delta\VOUT| = \Delta\VSGP/G$. Each specification
gives a lower bound on $G$:
PSRR (Proposition~\ref{T-prop:psrr}): $G \ge <<F['s4']['G_req']['psrr']|.1f>>$;
load regulation: $G \ge <<F['s4']['G_req']['load']|.1f>>$;
line regulation (Corollary~\ref{T-cor:static}c): $G \ge <<F['s4']['G_req']['line']|.1f>>$.
The PSRR is binding: $G_{min} = <<F['s4']['G_min']|.1f>>$.

\paragraph{Step 5 -- Transient budget} \cite[Eqs.~(2.10)--(2.12)]{rm_thesis}. By
Proposition~\ref{T-prop:budget}, the 50\,mV limit for the $\Delta I = <<F['s5']['dI']*1e3|.1f>>$\,mA step allows a response time
\[
\Delta t_{1,max} = \frac{\CL\,(\Delta V_{tr} - \Delta I\,\RESR)}{\Delta I} = <<F['s5']['dt1_max']*1e6|.2f>>\,\mu\text{s}.
\]
The loop reaction time $1/(2\pi\fu)$ and the gate slew time \eqref{T-eq:tsr} must fit in this budget.
Steps~6 and~7 check both.

\paragraph{Step 6 -- Gain stage} \cite[Section~2.4]{rm_thesis}, \cite[Section~IV]{rm_jssc}.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),itemsep=0.2ex]
\item \emph{Common-mode range around $\VREF$.} For a PMOS pair with an NMOS mirror,
$V_{IC,max} = V_{IN,min} - V_{SD,sat5} - V_{SG1} = <<FA['vic_max']|.3f>>$\,V and
$V_{IC,min} = V_{GS3} - |V_{TH1}| = <<FA['vic_min']|.3f>>$\,V. $\VREF = <<SP['VREF']|.1f>>$\,V lies inside.
\item \emph{Output swing.} The gain stage must move the buffer over the whole range from $M_P$ almost off
to full drive \cite[Section~IV]{rm_jssc}. For this topology that is the window of
Proposition~\ref{T-prop:window}: $<<FA['vsgb_lo']|.3f>>\,\text{V} \le \VSGB \le <<FA['vsgb_hi']|.3f>>$\,V. Its centre,
$\VSGB = <<FA['vsgb']|.2f>>$\,V, sets $V_{OV,B} = <<FA['vov_b']|.2f>>$\,V.
\item \emph{Gain and channel lengths.} With $V_{OV1} = 0.1$\,V, $\Aea = (2/V_{OV1})/(\lambda_p+\lambda_n)$
(Lemma~\ref{T-lem:ota}). By Proposition~\ref{T-prop:pea}, $\Aea\,p_{EA}$ is fixed for a given $L_{12}$ and
falls as $L_{12}$ grows, so any gain above the need costs bandwidth. The rule is therefore: the
shortest $L_{12}$, then the shortest $L_{34}$, whose square-law $G$ exceeds $G_{min}$ by 6\,dB. The margin
covers the known optimism of the square law ($g_m/I_D$ too high, $\lambda$ too low; Fig.~\ref{fig:gmid},
Section~\ref{sec:gap}), which iterations v1 and v2 measured. Table~\ref{tab:len} lists the candidates;
the rule selects $L_{12} = <<F['s6_lengths']['chosen']['L12']|d>>\,\mu$m, $L_{34} = <<F['s6_lengths']['chosen']['L34']|d>>\,\mu$m, with
$\Aea = <<FA['A_ea']|.0f>>$ and $G = <<FA['G']|.1f>>$.
\item \emph{Current.} By Proposition~\ref{T-prop:pea}, $p_{EA}$ does not depend on $I_{D1}$, so the
bandwidth requirement of \cite[Section~2.4]{rm_thesis} does not fix it; $I_{D1} = 20\,\mu$A per side
is a budget choice. Then $p_{EA} = <<FA['p_ea']/1e6|.2f>>$\,MHz (Pass~A).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering\small
\caption{Step~6c, square-law gain for each pair of channel lengths ($V_{OV1} = 0.1$\,V, $\Ab = <<FA['A_b']|.3f>>$).
The first row that meets $G \ge 2G_{min} = <<2*F['s4']['G_min']|.1f>>$ in the order $(L_{12}, L_{34})$ is chosen.}
\label{tab:len}
\begin{tabular}{cccccc}
\toprule
$L_{12}$ ($\mu$m) & $L_{34}$ ($\mu$m) & $\Aea$ & $G$ & $G/G_{min}$ & meets rule\\
\midrule
<<' \\\\ '.join(f"{c['L12']} & {c['L34']} & {c['A_ea']:.0f} & {c['G']:.1f} & {c['margin']:.2f} & {'yes' if c['ok'] else 'no'}" for c in F['s6_lengths']['candidates'])>>\\
