%===================================================================================
\section{Simulation}\label{sec:sim}

\paragraph{Method.} The loop gain uses the break of Proposition~\ref{T-prop:T}. A $10^9$\,H inductor
from the divider tap to the gate of $M_1$ closes the loop at DC, and a $10^3$\,F capacitor injects the
AC test voltage at the gate. Because the gate draws no current, this measures \eqref{T-eq:T} exactly
for $f \gg 1/(2\pi\sqrt{LC})$. The PSRR test puts an AC source on $\VIN$ with the loop closed. Line,
load and dropout are DC sweeps, and the load step is a transient run with 1\,$\mu$s edges.

\paragraph{Static output equation.} Insert the simulated operating-point voltages
and the simulated $\Aea = g_{m1}/(g_{ds2}+g_{ds4})$ into \eqref{T-eq:static}:
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0.1ex]
\item Light load: $\varepsilon = <<D4['light']['eps']*1e3|.0f>>$\,mV, so the formula gives
$\VOUT = <<D4['light']['vout_pred']|.4f>>$\,V. Simulation: <<D4['light']['vout_sim']|.4f>>\,V.
\item Heavy load: $\varepsilon = <<D4['heavy']['eps']*1e3|.0f>>$\,mV, so the formula gives
<<D4['heavy']['vout_pred']|.4f>>\,V. Simulation: <<D4['heavy']['vout_sim']|.4f>>\,V.
\end{itemize}
The equation therefore explains both the $\pm 5$\,mV output error and its change of sign with load.

\subsection{Design iterations}\label{sec:iter}
