\subsection{Hand calculation versus simulation}\label{sec:gap}
\begin{table}[H]
\centering\small
\caption{Final design at full load: the two passes of Section~\ref{sec:hand} and the simulation of v4.
Pass~A uses square-law parameters; Pass~B uses $g_m/I_D$ and $\lambda$ read at the v3 operating point.}
\label{tab:gap}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Quantity (full load) & Pass A & Pass B & Simulated (v4)\\
\midrule
$g_{m1}/I_{D1}$ (V$^{-1}$) & <<FA['gmid1']|.0f>> & <<FB['gmid1']|.1f>> & <<D4['heavy']['gm_over_id1']|.1f>>\\
$\lambda_{eff}$ of $M_2$ / $M_4$ (V$^{-1}$) & <<FA['lam2']|.3f>> / <<FA['lam4']|.3f>> & <<FB['lam2']|.3f>> / <<FB['lam4']|.3f>> & <<D4['heavy']['lam2']|.3f>> / <<D4['heavy']['lam4']|.3f>>\\
$\Aea$ & <<FA['A_ea']|.0f>> & <<FB['A_ea']|.0f>> & <<D4['heavy']['A_ea']|.0f>>\\
$G$ = PSRR$_{DC}$ (dB) & <<FA['psrr_dc_dB']|.1f>> & <<FB['psrr_dc_dB']|.1f>> & <<S4['psrr']['50mA']['dc']|.1f>>\\
$\fu$ (kHz) & <<FA['fu']/1e3|.0f>> & <<FB['fu']/1e3|.0f>> & <<S4['loop']['50mA']['UGF']/1e3|.0f>>\\
PM, $\RESR = 10$\,m$\Omega$ (deg) & -- & <<FB['PM_esr']|.1f>> & <<S4['loop']['50mA']['PM']|.1f>>\\
PM, $\RESR = 0$ (deg) & -- & <<FB['PM_esr0']|.1f>> & <<S4['esr']['50mA'][0][1]|.1f>>\\
Load regulation (mV) & <<FA['loadreg']*1e3|.1f>> & <<FB['loadreg']*1e3|.1f>> & <<S4['loadreg']['dV']*1e3|.1f>>\\
$I_Q$ ($\mu$A) & -- & <<F['s8']['Iq']*1e6|.0f>> & <<S4['iq']['at_1p8']*1e6|.1f>>\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Pass~A misses in two square-law inputs; the formulas themselves hold.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item \emph{$g_m/I_D$.} The input pair works at $\ov \approx 0.08$\,V, in moderate inversion, where
$g_m/I_D$ saturates toward $1/(nV_T)$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:gmid}) instead of following $2/\ov$. The simulated
value is <<D4['heavy']['gm_over_id1']|.1f>> against 20, about $<<db(20/D4['heavy']['gm_over_id1'])|.1f>>$\,dB of gain.
\item \emph{$\lambda$.} The extraction was done at $\ov = 0.2$\,V and $|V_{DS}| = 0.6$--1.2\,V. In the circuit,
$M_2$ has a smaller $\ov$ and $M_4$ sits at $V_{DS} = <<D4['heavy']['vea']|.2f>>$\,V, close to saturation,
where $g_{ds}$ is larger. The effective $\lambda$ values are higher than the table values.
\end{enumerate}
Pass~B puts the operating-point values into the same formulas and predicts the phase margin of v4 to
within 1$^\circ$, the loop gain to within 1\,dB, $\fu$ to within <<abs(FB['fu']/S4['loop']['50mA']['UGF']-1)*100|.0f>>\,\% and the quiescent current to
within 1\,$\mu$A. Because Pass~A overestimates $G$, it overestimates $\fu$ and finds no buffer current that reaches the
PM target; the second pass is what makes the buffer sizing usable.

The same mechanism affects the pass device. At full load $V_{SD} = 0.30$\,V is close to
$V_{DS,sat} = <<-O4['heavy']['@mp[vdsat]'] if O4['heavy']['@mp[vdsat]']<0 else O4['heavy']['@mp[vdsat]']|.2f>>$\,V,
so $g_{ds,P} = <<D4['heavy']['gdsp']*1e3|.0f>>$\,mS against $\lambda I = <<P['p_L0.18']['lam']*SP['IL_MAX']*1e3|.0f>>$\,mS. This lowers
$R_o$ and the DC loop gain (<<S4['loop']['50mA']['T0_dB']|.1f>>\,dB simulated against
<<V3['heavy']['T0_dB']|.1f>>\,dB by the square law), but it does not move $\fu$, which is independent of $R_o$
(Proposition~\ref{T-prop:fu}).

\subsection{Corners}\label{sec:corners}
The model file has no process corners, so the checks cover $\VIN$ and temperature only
(script \texttt{sim\_extra.py}).
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0.2ex]
\item \emph{Stability.} For $\VIN\in\{1.7,1.8,1.9\}$\,V, $T\in\{0,27,85\}\,^\circ$C and both load ends, the
minimum PM is <<C4['pm_min']|.1f>>$^\circ$ at $\RESR = 10$\,m$\Omega$, and <<C4['pm_esr0_min']|.1f>>$^\circ$ with
$\RESR = 0$ (at <<C4['pm_esr0_at']['vin']|.1f>>\,V, <<C4['pm_esr0_at']['temp']|d>>\,$^\circ$C).
\item \emph{Output voltage.} $\VOUT$ stays within <<C4['vout_min']|.4f>>--<<C4['vout_max']|.4f>>\,V over all corners.
\item \emph{PSRR, $\VIN\ge1.8$\,V.} The worst value is <<C4['psrr_nom_min']|.1f>>\,dB at
<<C4['psrr_nom_min_at']['vin']|.1f>>\,V, <<C4['psrr_nom_min_at']['temp']|d>>\,$^\circ$C,
<<C4['psrr_nom_min_at']['il']*1e3|.1f>>\,mA. This is just below the 30\,dB specification, which is stated
at the nominal point; the cause is the remaining headroom limit of Proposition~\ref{T-prop:window} at high $\VIN$.
\item \emph{PSRR, $\VIN = 1.7$\,V.} At full load this is the dropout region, and the PSRR falls to
<<C4['psrr_low_min']|.1f>>\,dB at 85\,$^\circ$C. Corollary~\ref{T-cor:dropout_psrr} explains it: at the
27\,$^\circ$C dropout point, the simulated ratio $\gdsP/\gmP$ is already <<D4['dropout']['ratio']|.2f>>
(against <<D4['heavy']['ratio']|.2f>> at 1.8\,V), and it grows further when hot. Datasheets specify PSRR
with $\VIN$ well above $\VOUT+V_{DO}$ for this reason.
\end{itemize}

%===================================================================================
\section{Limitations and next steps}\label{sec:limits}
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0.2ex]
\item $\VREF$ and $I_{REF}$ are ideal. A bandgap reference and its PSRR would add to the supply path.
\item Only the typical model is available: no process corners and no mismatch (Monte Carlo) analysis.
Random offset of the input pair adds to $\varepsilon$ in \eqref{T-eq:static}.
\item The design is schematic-level only. Layout parasitics, bond-wire inductance in series with $\CL$, and
the pass-device metal resistance are not included.
\item Not covered: start-up, current limit and short-circuit protection, noise, and the soft-start of $\VREF$.
\item The PSRR is limited to about $G$ by the ground-referenced gate (Corollary~\ref{T-cor:tradeoff}). Possible
improvements are a second EA gain stage with its own compensation, or a gate drive that tracks $\VIN$.
\end{itemize}

\appendix
\section{Reproducing the results}
The scripts, results and document sources are in the GitHub repository
\url{https://github.com/NguyenVuQuocMinh/cmos-ldo-regulator-180nm}. The 0.18\,$\mu$m model file is not
redistributed there; place a BSIM3 model with \texttt{.model NMOS} and \texttt{.model PMOS} as
\texttt{sim/cmos180.lib}, and set the environment variable \texttt{NGSPICE} to the ngspice console
executable if it is not at the default path. Run from \texttt{scripts/}:
\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0.1ex]
\item \texttt{python char\_devices.py}: device parameters (Table~\ref{tab:par}, Fig.~\ref{fig:gmid}).
\item \texttt{python design.py}: earlier iterations, writes \texttt{sizes\_v1/v2/v3.json} and \texttt{hand.json}.
\item For v1--v3: set \texttt{LDO\_SIZES=sizes\_vN.json} and \texttt{LDO\_VER=vN}, then run
\texttt{sim\_op.py}, \texttt{sim\_all.py} and \texttt{sim\_extra.py}.
\item \texttt{python design\_flow.py}: the procedure of Section~\ref{sec:hand} (Pass~B reads the v3 operating
point), writes \texttt{sizes\_v4.json} and \texttt{hand\_flow.json}. Then run the three simulation scripts for v4.
\item \texttt{python render.py <path-to-tectonic.exe>}: fills this document with the numbers and builds the PDF.
\end{enumerate}
The netlist is generated by \texttt{ldo\_netlist.py} from the sizes file. The repository folder
\texttt{netlists/} holds the final circuit (v4) with its operating-point, loop-gain, PSRR and
load-step test benches; each runs directly with \texttt{ngspice\_con -b} once the model file is in place.

